A||||15||2birds||Tire upgrades/fit???||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||01-09-2000||03:28 AM||||I'm going to be replacing my tires this summer, and I'm curious as to what will fit without throwing off the speedo too bad, causing tire rub, or screwing up the scrub radius (don't understand the theory too well, but I'm  willing to learn).  I have an '86 GT with the factory 15x7's, lowered 1.5" with springs.  I currently have BFG Comp T/A HR4's, which I've been very pleased with.  I have 225/60 out back and 215/60 up front.  Since I lowered it, I have a bit of rub at full lock with the 215's, but with the stock 205's I didn't have a problem.  Thanks in advance for any suggestions. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||01-09-2000||05:23 AM||||<BR><B>2birds</B>, if you use the search function for "tires", you'll find a ton of stuff posted here. The last thread we had on this topic was:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001775.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001775.html</A> <P>Here's a link from that same thread to another site that has a helpful chart about tire sizes:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html</A> <P>||||reg||14
Z||000002||Dave85||01-09-2000||01:59 PM||||What do you think? BF has <B>265/50/14</B>. Just wondering if they would fit on the back end.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||02:20 PM||||Your speedo reading will be off by almost 5.5% according to the miata chart.  What width was the rim on the back of a pre-'88?||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||01-09-2000||05:59 PM||||Thanks, Patrick.  I was on vacation in PA when that thread went through.  I'm really looking for first-hand experience from anyone running the factory 86-87 GT wheels (15x7).  I love the factory wheels.  I've toyed with the idea of using some of the five spoke Grand Am or Cavalier 16" wheels, but most are only 6 or 6.5" wide.  As the thread shows, actual sizes vary among different brands, so the tire calcs are just an educated guess .     ||||reg||14
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||08:55 PM||||2-birds,<P>They are much closer than an educated guess.  Although there will be slight variance between manufacturers it should be essentially negligable.  Otherwise all odometers would be useless. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000006||2birds||01-09-2000||11:35 PM||||The diameter is not the problem.  I can check that with the tire calc.  Thee 225/60/15 I have in back now are .5 inch taller with no ill effect.  I'm mostly concerned with clearance from the sidewall to the strut in back, and the fender lip up front, tires not sticking out too far, wheel bearing damage, general aesthetics.  I don't want to screw it up by going too wide.  Tasteful, practical improvement is the goal.  ||||reg||12
Z||000007||batboy||01-10-2000||12:22 AM||||You can probably easily go up to a 235/60/15 in the back, but if you have lowered the front, a 205/60/15 is about as big as you can go in a 60 series with a 15" wheel.<P>I've done some figuring and measuring. If I use rear coil-overs I should be able to squeeze in a pair of 305/50/15 BFG Euro T/A tires in the rear of the Batmobile and maybe 225/50/15 in the front.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shiner||01-10-2000||04:28 AM||||305's?  Are you f***ing nuts?!?  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000009||Monkeyman||01-10-2000||05:39 AM||||I wonder what a Fiero would look like with 385/30/17s on the back? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (With the proper susp mods, of course!)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||01-10-2000||07:28 AM||||On my Lambo kit I'm running 295/50 15 on 10X15 Centerlines 4" backspace with Ryane coil-overs everything fits fine. But I don't think this setup would under the Fiero fenders||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||01-11-2000||08:24 PM||||Supposedly, those 305/50/15 tires are only 1.5" taller and about 0.5" wider than the 245/60/14 tires I have on the rear right now. I still have a lot of room in the wheel well except at the strut tower. That's why I need the coil-overs if I go with anything wider. Yeah, I might be a little nuts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I do lots of winter dreaming, doesn't always mean I do everything I dreamed about when spring rolls around.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||01-12-2000||07:21 AM||||The 295/50 15s are 12"wide and 28"tall. They were quite a bit different than the stock tire from my 85GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||01-15-2000||08:59 PM||||Reality caught up with me. I decided on BF Goodrich Comp T/A R1 (Z speed rated) on 15" wheels (which I haven't got yet). Rear size will be 255/60/15 and up front will be 205/60/15. I still may have to use rear coil-overs. You guys were right, I goofed up my calculations somewhere, those 305/50/15 tires would never of fit under the stock rear fenders without modification.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||01-16-2000||11:16 PM||||Sounds like a good combo with your drivetrain setup,you do have a rear swaybar on the Batmobile,huh?||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||01-17-2000||12:19 AM||||No rear sway bar yet, but that's a high priority. Ratt, one of the other members gave me instructions how to mount a front Fiero sway bar in the rear. If I can get a good set of used wheels cheap enough, then I'll be able to afford rear coil-overs. I've already filed my taxes and have a nice refund coming back. Time to stock up on car parts for upcoming spring projects, like a pair of V8 aluminum heads for the Batmobile.||||reg||1
